<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="biostats-notes-4-2-confidence-intervals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Confidence Intervals</title>
    
    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Identify and write null and alternative hypotheses in words and symbols. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-confidence-interval">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>\hat{p}</m> be a point estimate for a parameter <m>p</m> computed from a sample of size <m>n</m>. If we are <m>(1-\alpha)</m>% confident that <m>p</m> falls within a certain range of values, we call the range of values the <alert>confidence interval</alert> with <alert>confidence level</alert> <m>(1-\alpha)</m>%, or <alert> significance level </alert> <m>\alpha</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Keep in mind that there is rarely every a way to truley know the parameter we are after. The best we can typically do is to give a range that we suspect it falls in. The range is not necessarily correct, but we have some measureable way to determine how likely it does. 
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that a sample <m>n=36</m> is drawn from a norammly-distributed random variable <m>X</m> with standard deviation <m>\sigma = 3</m>. It is discovered that <m>\bar{x}=20</m>. Build a 95% confidence interval for <m>\mu</m>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                We want to find <m>a,b</m> such that <m> a \leq \mu \leq b </m>. It is not possible to know <m>a,b</m> for certain, but we can build a reasonable estimate using information about the sampling distribution. Recall CLT tells us that <m> \xbar \sim N(\mu, 0.5) </m>. Draw a cumul. prob. diagram in <m>X</m> space. Move it to <m>Z</m> space. The corresponding thresholds are the ends of the confidence interval. 
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

